<div class="tab-content active" id="notes-2-3">

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Equilibrium</h2>
    <p>The <strong>equilibrium price</strong> is the price at which the quantity demanded equals the quantity supplied (Qd = Qs). At this price, the market clears — everyone who wants to buy or sell at that price can do so.</p>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/market-equilibrium.svg" alt="Demand and supply curves crossing at equilibrium price Pe and quantity Qe" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Equilibrium occurs where demand meets supply, at price Pe and quantity Qe.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Getting to equilibrium</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Situation</th><th>What happens</th><th>Result</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Price too high → surplus (Qs &gt; Qd)</td>
          <td>Producers lower prices to clear their inventory</td>
          <td>Price falls, Qd rises, Qs falls, until Qd = Qs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Price too low → shortage (Qd &gt; Qs)</td>
          <td>Consumers bid up the price</td>
          <td>Price rises, Qd falls, Qs rises, until Qd = Qs</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/surplus-and-shortage.svg" alt="A price above equilibrium creating excess supply (surplus) and a price below equilibrium creating excess demand (shortage)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Above Pe: a surplus (excess supply). Below Pe: a shortage (excess demand).</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Functions of the Price Mechanism</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Function</th><th>How it works</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Signalling</strong></td><td>Price changes send signals to producers and consumers about relative scarcity or abundance</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Incentives</strong></td><td>Higher prices incentivise producers to produce more; lower prices incentivise consumers to buy more</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rationing (price rationing)</strong></td><td>When a good is scarce, a higher price rations it among those willing and able to pay. Non-price rationing uses other mechanisms (queues, permits, etc.).</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem;">These functions are how the price mechanism leads to <strong>allocative efficiency</strong>: resources flow toward the goods consumers most prefer, without any central direction.</p>

    <div class="callout key-term">
      <div class="callout-title">The three work together</div>
      <p>A shortage pushes the price up. The higher price is a <strong>signal</strong> that the good has become scarce relative to what people want. That signal <strong>incentivises</strong> producers to supply more and consumers to buy less. And while the adjustment happens, the higher price <strong>rations</strong> the limited quantity to those willing and able to pay it. One number does all three jobs at once.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Planned versus market economies</h3>
    <p>In a market economy, no single person decides what gets produced. Price does the coordinating. Nobody instructs a street seller to stand on a corner with raincoats the moment it starts raining: the price people will pay for a raincoat at that moment tells her, and the profit on offer makes it worth her while.</p>
    <p>In a planned economy, someone has to make that decision without the signal. It is worth asking what information a planner must guess at that a price would have told them automatically:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>How urgently consumers want this good, and how much they want it <em>relative to</em> everything else</li>
      <li>How scarce the inputs are, and what else those inputs could have made</li>
      <li>Which producers can make the good at the lowest cost</li>
      <li>How all of this has changed since yesterday</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is the core of the argument for markets: not that planners are foolish, but that the information they would need is scattered across millions of people and is never written down anywhere. Price is the summary.</p>

    <h3>The three economic questions, on the graph</h3>
    <p>The demand and supply diagram is not just a picture of one market. It answers the three basic economic questions.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Question</th><th>Where the answer sits on the diagram</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>What, and how much, gets produced?</strong></td>
          <td>The equilibrium quantity Qe. Goods that consumers value highly relative to the cost of making them support a high price, which draws resources in. Goods nobody wants cannot cover their costs, and resources leave.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>How is it produced?</strong></td>
          <td>The supply curve. Every producer receives the same price Pe, so the ones with lower costs earn more producer surplus. Any method that lowers cost is rewarded, which is what pushes production towards the cheapest available technique.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Who ends up with it?</strong></td>
          <td>Rationing. Everyone whose willingness to pay is at or above Pe buys the good and gains consumer surplus. Everyone below Pe does not buy. The good goes to those willing <em>and able</em> to pay, which is not the same as those who need it most.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Writing the Explanation</h2>
    <p>Knowing what happens is not the same as being able to write it. Most marks on this topic are lost not because students get the direction wrong, but because they skip steps in the causal chain, or blur demand with quantity demanded. Use the checklist to mark your own paragraph, then compare it with the model answers.</p>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Paragraph checklist</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Stated the price level, compared to equilibrium</li>
        <li>Identified the surplus or shortage as the imbalance between Qs and Qd <em>at that price</em></li>
        <li>Kept D and S separate from Qd and Qs</li>
        <li>Used <strong>shift</strong> only for the curve change, and <strong>movement along</strong> for the response to price</li>
        <li>Explained <em>why</em> the price changed, not just that it did (bid up by buyers, lowered to clear a surplus)</li>
        <li>Used the words <strong>signal</strong> and <strong>incentivised</strong> in the explanation</li>
        <li>Showed the full sequence: imbalance, price pressure, change in Qd and Qs, new equilibrium</li>
        <li>Used graph terms throughout: P&#8321;, P&#8322;, Q&#8321;, Q&#8322;, D, S</li>
        <li>Stated the new equilibrium in both price and quantity</li>
        <li>For a shift: kept the <strong>cause</strong> (the shift) separate from the <strong>adjustment</strong> (the price mechanism)</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p>Where the graph carries numbers, include them beside the labels, e.g. P&#8321; = $5.</p>

    <h3>Model answer 1: a price above equilibrium</h3>
    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/surplus-and-shortage.svg" alt="A price above equilibrium creating excess supply (surplus) and a price below equilibrium creating excess demand (shortage)" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>At P&#8321;, above Pe, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded: a surplus.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
      The price is at P&#8321;, above equilibrium (Pe). At P&#8321;, Qs exceeds Qd: a surplus, which is a signal. Sellers are incentivised to lower the price to clear stock. As price falls, Qd rises and Qs falls, a movement along both curves, in line with the laws of demand and supply. This continues until price reaches Pe, where Qd = Qs at Qe. Overall, the price started above equilibrium, so the surplus pushed price down until the market cleared.
    </div>

    <h3>Model answer 2: an increase in supply</h3>
    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/increase-in-supply-new-equilibrium.svg" alt="Supply shifting right from S1 to S2 against a fixed demand curve, creating a surplus at the original price P1 and a new equilibrium at P2 and Q2" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Supply shifts from S&#8321; to S&#8322;. At the original price P&#8321; there is a surplus (red), which pushes the market to a new equilibrium at P&#8322; and Q&#8322;.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
      A reduction in labour costs means that producers are more willing and able to produce at any given price. Supply shifts right, from S&#8321; to S&#8322;. At the original price P&#8321;, quantity supplied now exceeds quantity demanded: a surplus, which is a signal to producers. Sellers are incentivised to lower the price to clear it. As price falls, quantity demanded rises and quantity supplied falls. This continues until price reaches P&#8322;, where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at Q&#8322;. Overall, supply shifted, leading to a lower price and a higher quantity.
    </div>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">Keep the cause separate from the adjustment</div>
      <p>In model answer 2, the fall in labour costs is the <strong>cause</strong>: it shifts the curve. The fall in price is the <strong>adjustment</strong>: it is how the market responds to the surplus that the shift created. Writing "costs fell so the price fell" collapses the two and skips every step that earns marks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Consumer and Producer Surplus</h2>

    <h3>The big idea</h3>
    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Consumer Surplus</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Different consumers value the same good differently. Some would pay far more than the market price; some only just buy it because the price equals what it is worth to them. In the market, everyone pays the same price. Consumer surplus is all the extra benefit that buyers get from paying less than the maximum they would have been willing to pay — the "bonus value."</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem; margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>On a diagram:</strong> the area under the demand curve but above the price line.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="content-card">
        <div class="content-card-title">Producer Surplus</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Different producers are willing to sell at different prices. Some would sell very cheaply; others only sell if the price is higher. In the market, everyone receives the same price. Producer surplus is all the extra benefit producers gain by selling at a price above the minimum they would have accepted — their cost.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem; margin-top:0.5rem;"><strong>On a diagram:</strong> the area above the supply curve but below the price line.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="graph-figure">
      <img src="/ibecon/graphs/micrographs/consumer-producer-surplus.svg" alt="Consumer surplus shaded above the price line under demand, producer surplus shaded below the price line above supply" loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Consumer surplus (blue) lies under demand and above Pe; producer surplus (amber) lies above supply and below Pe.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>Technical definition</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Consumer surplus (CS)</strong> = Total Benefit (area under demand curve up to Q) − Amount Spent (P × Q)</li>
      <li><strong>Producer surplus (PS)</strong> = Total Revenue (P × Q) − Total Cost (area under supply curve up to Q)</li>
      <li>For a linear demand and supply: CS and PS are triangles → area = ½ × base × height</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Allocative efficiency</h3>
    <p>Social surplus (CS + PS) is maximised at the competitive market equilibrium, where <strong>MB = MC</strong> (marginal benefit equals marginal cost). This is the condition for allocative efficiency.</p>
    <p>If output is above or below the equilibrium, there is a <strong>deadweight loss (DWL)</strong> — a triangle of social surplus that is lost. DWL represents free utility that has been destroyed by not being at the allocatively efficient output.</p>
  </div>

</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="worked-2-3">
  <h2>Worked Example — Consumer and Producer Surplus <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
  <p>At market equilibrium: Price = $5, Quantity = 500 units. The demand curve reaches the price axis at $10 and the supply curve originates from the origin (P = $0 at Q = 0).</p>

  <h3>Consumer Surplus</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    CS = ½ × base × height = ½ × 500 × ($10 − $5) = ½ × 500 × 5 = <strong>$1,250</strong>
  </div>

  <h3>Producer Surplus</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    PS = ½ × base × height = ½ × 500 × ($5 − $0) = ½ × 500 × 5 = <strong>$1,250</strong>
  </div>

  <h3>Social Surplus</h3>
  <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
    Social surplus = CS + PS = $1,250 + $1,250 = <strong>$2,500</strong><br>
    This is maximised at the competitive equilibrium. Any deviation creates a DWL.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="tab-content" id="practice-2-3">

  <div class="callout exam-tip">
    <div class="callout-title">How to answer these</div>
    <p>For each scenario, draw a diagram showing the change in demand <em>or</em> supply and the resulting change in equilibrium. Then choose three and write the full explanation in words.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem;">Watch your language throughout: shift of the curve versus movement along it, surplus versus shortage, and demand or supply versus quantity demanded or quantity supplied.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Changes in equilibrium</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">1. Bread; a rise in wages increases the cost of producing bread.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply shifts left.</strong> At the original price there is a shortage, so price is bid up. New equilibrium: <strong>higher price, lower quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">2. Solar panels; the government introduces a subsidy for solar panels.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply shifts right.</strong> At the original price there is a surplus, so price falls. New equilibrium: <strong>lower price, higher quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">3. Cars; the price of petrol rises sharply.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand shifts left.</strong> Petrol is a complement to cars. At the original price there is a surplus, so price falls. New equilibrium: <strong>lower price, lower quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">4. Smartphones; advances in technology reduce the cost of producing smartphones.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply shifts right.</strong> New equilibrium: <strong>lower price, higher quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">5. Rice; a severe drought destroys a large portion of the rice harvest.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Supply shifts left.</strong> At the original price there is a shortage, so consumers bid the price up. New equilibrium: <strong>higher price, lower quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">6. Bubble tea; consumer incomes rise in Vietnam.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand shifts right</strong> (a normal good). New equilibrium: <strong>higher price, higher quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">7. Tablets; a fall in the price of laptops affects the demand for tablets.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand shifts left.</strong> Laptops are a substitute, and they have become cheaper. New equilibrium: <strong>lower price, lower quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">8. Chicken; a rise in the price of beef affects the chicken market.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <strong>Demand shifts right.</strong> Beef is a substitute, and it has become more expensive. New equilibrium: <strong>higher price, higher quantity</strong>.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Write it properly</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">9. A price is set <strong>below</strong> equilibrium. Write a full paragraph explaining how the market returns to equilibrium. Work through the whole causal chain: the price, the imbalance, how buyers respond, the change in Qd and Qs, the new equilibrium. Mark it against the checklist in the notes.</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          The price is at P&#8322;, below equilibrium (Pe). At P&#8322;, Qd exceeds Qs: a shortage, which is a signal. Buyers compete for the limited quantity available and bid the price up, and sellers are incentivised to raise it. As price rises, Qd falls and Qs rises, a movement along both curves, in line with the laws of demand and supply. This continues until price reaches Pe, where Qd = Qs at Qe. Overall, the price started below equilibrium, so the shortage pushed price up until the market cleared.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">10. Write a full paragraph explaining what happens when <strong>demand increases</strong>. Keep the cause (the shift) separate from the adjustment (the price mechanism).</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          A rise in consumer incomes means consumers are more willing and able to buy at any given price. Demand shifts right, from D&#8321; to D&#8322;. At the original price P&#8321;, quantity demanded now exceeds quantity supplied: a shortage, which is a signal to producers. Buyers bid the price up, and producers are incentivised to supply more. As price rises, quantity demanded falls and quantity supplied rises, a movement along both curves. This continues until price reaches P&#8322;, where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at Q&#8322;. Overall, demand shifted, leading to a higher price and a higher quantity.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Case study: Vietnam cuts fuel taxes</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">
        <p style="font-weight:400; margin-bottom:0.75rem;">In March 2026, escalating tensions in the Strait of Hormuz pushed crude oil prices above USD 100 per barrel. The Vietnamese government responded by cutting fuel taxes to zero: environmental protection tax reduced to VND 0 per litre, special consumption tax set at 0%, and no VAT declaration or payment on petrol (excluding ethanol), diesel and aviation fuel. The measures ran from midnight on 26 March 2026 to the end of 15 April 2026, at an estimated cost of around VND 7.2 trillion per month in lost budget revenue. The government described it as an urgent measure to stabilise the domestic fuel market and ensure national energy security. <a href="https://moit.gov.vn/en/news/official-tax-cuts-on-petrol-and-oil-products-announced.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source: Ministry of Industry and Trade</a>.</p>
        11. Evaluate this policy response to rising petrol prices. Consider the role of prices in signalling, incentives and rationing, as well as equity issues. <span class="exercise-marks">[15 marks]</span>
      </div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer guidance</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          <p><strong>The diagram.</strong> The world price shock shifts the supply of petrol in Vietnam left, raising price and lowering quantity. Removing the taxes shifts supply back to the right, since indirect taxes act as a cost on producers. The net effect is a domestic price lower than it would otherwise have been, and a quantity higher than it would otherwise have been.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><strong>Against the policy, using the three functions.</strong> The high world price is a <em>signal</em> that oil has become genuinely scarcer. Suppressing the domestic price muffles that signal. It removes the <em>incentive</em> for consumers to cut back, switch to public transport or delay non-essential journeys, and it removes the incentive for producers and importers to seek out alternative supply. It also weakens <em>rationing</em>: with a genuinely scarcer good held at an artificially low price, consumption stays high and shortages or queues become more likely. Vietnam still has to buy the oil at the world price, so the underlying scarcity has not gone away, it has simply been shifted onto the state budget at roughly VND 7.2 trillion per month.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><strong>For the policy.</strong> Petrol demand is highly price inelastic in the short run, so the signalling and incentive argument is weaker than it looks: consumers cannot switch quickly, and a sharp price rise mostly transfers income away from households rather than changing behaviour. Fuel is an input to almost everything, so a spike feeds through to transport and food prices, risking cost-push inflation across the economy. A time-limited measure (three weeks here) is a reasonable way to smooth a shock that may prove temporary, rather than a permanent subsidy.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><strong>Equity.</strong> Fuel spending takes a larger share of a low-income household's budget, so a price spike is regressive in its impact, and cutting the tax protects those households. But the benefit of a fuel tax cut goes to whoever buys the most fuel, which is not the poorest: a household with two cars gains far more than one with a motorbike, and someone with no vehicle at all gains nothing directly. A targeted cash transfer would deliver the same protection to poor households at lower cost and without blunting the price signal. There is also an environmental equity point: the environmental protection tax exists to price in the external cost of burning fuel, and setting it to zero removes that correction at exactly the moment consumption is being encouraged.</p>
          <p style="margin-top:0.6rem;"><strong>A judgement.</strong> Defensible as a short, time-limited response to a sharp external shock, particularly given inelastic short-run demand and the inflation risk. Much harder to defend if extended, because the fiscal cost is large and recurring, the price signal stays suppressed, and the benefits are poorly targeted. The stated end date of 15 April 2026 is the feature that makes the policy most defensible.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Concept checks</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">12. A street seller was not told to sell raincoats on the night it rained. What told her? What does that show about how a market economy decides what and how much to produce?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          The price told her. Rain shifted demand for raincoats right, creating a shortage at the old price and pushing the price up. That higher price signalled that raincoats were now scarce relative to what people wanted, and the profit available incentivised her to supply them. No one held the information "it is raining and people want raincoats" and passed it down; the price carried it. This is how a market economy answers "what and how much" without a planner: resources move towards whatever currently commands a price above its cost of production.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">13. Prices ration goods to whoever is willing and able to pay. If a price cap is imposed, what replaces price as the rationing method?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          Something non-price has to do the rationing, because at the capped price quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied and the good still has to be allocated somehow. In practice: queues and waiting lists (rationing by time and patience), first come first served, seller favouritism or discrimination, formal rationing coupons, or parallel markets where the good is resold above the cap. Note that each of these has its own distributional effect, and none of them is obviously fairer than price. This links forward to 2.7 Role of Government.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">14. What information does a price actually communicate that a producer or consumer would not otherwise have?</div>
      <details class="answer">
        <summary>Show answer</summary>
        <div class="answer-content">
          A price summarises the combined judgement of everyone else in the market: how much other consumers want this good relative to alternatives, how scarce the inputs are, what those inputs could otherwise have produced, and how all of that has changed recently. No individual knows any of this directly. A producer deciding what to make does not need to know <em>why</em> a price rose, only that it did, and that it is now worth making more.
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exercises">
    <div class="exercises-title">Discussion</div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">15. Is a price a fact or an opinion? What is it actually made of?</div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">16. Prices ration goods to whoever is willing and able to pay. Is that the same as rationing goods to whoever needs them most? If not, when does the difference matter most?</div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercise">
      <div class="exercise-q">17. Would you rather live somewhere shortages are resolved by rising prices, or by queues? What is each one rewarding?</div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

